current bypass in the radial direction also results in a larger charging decay and loss. For example, the total charging time of the 32.35 T magnet was more than 15 h, which is not ideal for user magnets. Previous studies have shown that the turnto-turn contact resistance, which is the resistance in the radial direction in the coil or thickness direction in tape, is key for self-protection and charging loss. To date, at least nine studies have reported the measurement of turn-to-turn contact resistance in a short sample [5–14]. As the commercial tapes do not have sufficiently high resistance, more studies have focused on the effect of the turn-to-turn contact resistance with different insert materials, such as stain steel, brass, and copper, on the performance of the REBCO coils [14–25].
Although the popular process of increasing the turn-to-turn contact resistance includes adding insert materials, the critical engineering current density (Je) is significantly reduced. In addition, co-winding with stainless steel (SS), which is the most popular insert material, can increase the turn-to-turn contact resistance to 100 00–100 000 µΩ cm2. This could be too high for self-protection. According to the simple model from [26], the optimized turn-to-turn contact resistance should be at least one or two orders of magnitude lower than the SS co-winding value. In addition, unlike the charging decay and loss, the self-protection ability is not only dominated by the turn-to-turn contact resistance but is also closely related to the thermal conductivity of the REBCO tapes in both the circumferential and radial directions. However, in the past two decades, only seven studies have focused on measuring the thermal conductivity of commercial REBCO tapes [27–33], and only two of them have reported the thermal conductivity in the radial/turn-to-turn direction [32, 33]. In 2020, Zhang et al reported that turn-to-turn contact resistance can differ by more than one order of magnitude in different commercial tapes [34]. It was found that the chemical condition could be an essential factor affecting the resistance. Previous reports on turn-to-turn contact resistance have mainly focused on tapes from Superpower and SuNAM. Commercial REBCO tapes from other companies have been rarely mentioned.
In this study, we established the first database of commercial REBCO tapes on the resistance and thermal conductivity in both the radial and circumferential directions. We systematically investigated 20 different commercial REBCO tapes with different Cu stabilizer parameters. This database can help other scientists in their research on superconductor magnet design. Furthermore, by analyzing the relationship between the Cu stabilizer parameters and the resistance and thermal conductivity, the primary influencing factor was explored. Our final goal was to determine a method of obtaining the resistance and thermal conductivity that fit the criterion of different application scenarios, instead of using insert materials by scarifying the Je value.
2. Experimental
All commercial tapes were stored in a cabinet with controlled temperature and humidity in the case of any oxidation and contamination that could affect the contact resistance. The

resistance in the radial direction (in the coil) or thickness direction (in the tape), namely turn-to-turn contact resistivity, was measured using custom-built equipment in a liquid nitrogen bath [34]. The resistivity in the circumferential direction (in the coil) or length direction (in the tape) and thermal conductivity in both directions were measured using the physical properties measurement system (PPMS). The circumferential thermal conductivity was directly measured using one piece of tape, and the radial thermal conductivity was measured using the method reported in [32]. The roughness microstructure of the commercial REBCO tape surface was measured using atomic force microscopy (AFM) and the microstructure was measured using scanning electron microscopy (SEM). The mechanical properties were measured using an electromechanical universal testing machine (Instron CTM2200) at room temperature in a liquid nitrogen bath.
