The mechanical properties also have a significant influence on the thickness of the Cu layer. The Young’s modulus and yield strength at both room temperature and 77 K are shown in table 2, and the tensile curves of all the tapes are shown in figure S2. According to common knowledge, the mechanical strength of composite materials mainly depends on their composition. Except for SuNAM, all the tapes used Hastelloy as a substrate, and the thickness was also similar. Young’s modulus and yield strength mainly depend on the thickness of the Cu layer. Figure 1 shows the relationship between Young’s modulus and yield strength with the thickness of the Cu layer. As SCS 2–5 has a more complicated structure than the simple Cu layer, these were also included in this study. It was found that both Young’s modulus and yield strength decreased with increasing Cu layer thickness.

However, three points must be considered. First, it was found that at the thickness of approximately 40 µm, Young’s modulus (106–149 MPa at room temperature) and yield strength (320–890 MPa) varied significantly. Even after considering the effect of the different thicknesses of Ag and Hastelloy, the difference in Young’s modulus and yield strength still cannot be explained. Second, Young’s modulus decreased and the yield strength increased when the temperature reached 77 K from room temperature. However, the increase and decrease magnitudes were not the same in the samples and were not proportional to the thickness of the Cu layer. For example, samples SZ1, SZ2, and SZ3 showed exactly the same thickness parameters for all layers. However, Young’s moduli were 158.3, 151.9, and 158.8 at room temperature, but were 142.8, 131.3, and 134 at 77 K, respectively. Clearly, SZ3 showed a higher decrease than the other two samples. Third, SP3 has a significantly thicker Cu layer than SP2 but showed no change in yield strength at both room temperature and 77 K. This result contradicts previous literature from the Superpower company [36].
In addition, most of the tapes showed an increase in yield strength at a lower temperature (77 K). However, SCS2 and SCS4 showed a different patterns. The yield strength at 77 K was smaller than that at room temperature. The reason for this needs to be explored.

