3.2. Radial properties of different commercial REBCO tapes
The resistivity and thermal conductivity of 20 different commercial tapes in the radial (to the coil) or thickness (to the tape) directions were systematically investigated. Normally, the radial direction is also called the turn-to-turn direction, and the current bypass is one of the key points of the noinsulation REBCO coils during charging and local quenching. The resistivity in the radial direction determines the bypass ability. Additionally, as the heart transform is also important during the local quench, the thermal conductivity could also be critical.

3.2.1. Resistivity. Figure 2 shows the resistivity of 20 different commercial tapes in the radial direction under different pressures. It was found that the 20 REBCO tapes can be divided into four groups. SC1, SZ3, SOX1, and SOX2 showed high resistivity (500–1000 µΩ cm2) in the radial direction. SP2, SP3, and SCS1 formed the second group and showed resistivities in the range of 200–400 µΩ cm2. SCS2, SCS3, SCS4, and SCS5 showed extremely low resistivities in the range of 5–15 µΩ cm2. All the other nine REBCO tapes showed similar resistivities (40–200 µΩ cm2), which is consistent with the results from other studies. As SCS2, SCS3, SCS4, and SCS5 are metal laminating types, the outer surface also has the PbSn solder. Such an extremely low resistivity value has also been reported for SuNAM tapes, which also have solder on the surface [8]. Therefore, we deduced that the solder on the surface can dramatically decrease the resistivity in the radial direction. This could be useful if some part of the REBCO coil is required to have a much better bypass ability than other parts. A solder surface addition could be helpful, which may reveal some new coil structures. The effect of adding the solder surface is being studied currently.
Next, we discuss why SC1, SZ3, SOX1, SOX2, SP2, SP3, and SCS1 have higher resistivities and evaluate the relationship between resistivity and Cu layer parameters. As mentioned in a previous study, the current can flow in two ways [34]. According to the sketch map, there are three main parameters of the Cu layer that affect the resistivity, of which surface roughness is most important. According to the island contact model [37], a coarser surface should have a larger resistivity. Beyond the roughness, the thickness of the surface Cu layer (front and back) could also have an effect. Increasing the thickness of the Cu layer increased the body resistance of the Cu layer on the current path. The final factor was the Cu thickness on the side. As the current could flow along the path of the side Cu instead of directly going through the Hastelloy [35], the side thickness could also be important.
