3.3. Circumferential properties of different commercial REBCO tapes
In the REBCO coils, the radial bypass was the largest focal point. However, the current bypass along the circumferential direction cannot be completely ignored. Normally, the

resistivity and thermal conductivity of REBCO tapes in the circumferential direction are estimated using the theoretical value and thickness fraction of each layer. In this study, the resistivity and thermal conductivity of 20 different commercial tapes in the circumferential direction (to the coil) or length direction (to the tape) were systematically measured and investigated.
3.3.1. Resistivity. The resistivity was measured using the four-probe method from 300 K to 80 K. However, at approximately 90 K, the resistance was dominated by the superconductor layer, and only the data in the range of 100 K–300 K are shown in figure 5. It can be observed that the resistivity of the different tapes has a difference of more than an order of magnitude. Figures 5(c) and (d) show the relationship between the resistivity in the circumferential direction and the surface thickness of the Cu layer at 300 K and 100 K. We could not detect any trend between the resistivity and Cu layer thickness at either a low temperature (100 K) or near room temperature (300 K). Even when considering the thickness of the Ag layer, the data were not clear. In particular, for SP1, SP2, and SP3, the resistivity did not show any monotonous change, but the Cu layer thickness increased monotonously. These results reveal that beyond the thickness of the Cu layer, there could be other factors that affect the resistivity in the circumferential direction. The most important factor was the purity of the Cu layer. As most of the Cu layer is fabricated by

