The Wiedemann–Franz law quantitatively defines the correlation between the electrical and thermal conductivities in metals and is characterized by the Lorenz number, L = κρ/T. The predictions of the Wiedemann–Franz law are in good agreement with experiments at low temperatures and zero magnetic fields, provided that the phonon contribution to thermal transport is negligible and that electron scattering processes are elastic [38]. Figure 8 shows the measured and calculated values. The resistivity and thermal conductivity were measured in the temperature range of 100 K–300 K and 20 K– 200 K, respectively. Therefore, the data in the overlap temperature range of 100 K–200 K were compared in this study. It was found that the tapes, which have higher thermal conductivity, had a better agreement between the measured and calculated values. Although the absolute value was not very close, the ranking of the tapes was the same. The order from high to low was SCS4, SCS2, SP2, SP3, SP1, SC2, and FUJI. However, in the low thermal-conductor range, the ranking was completely different. This clearly shows that other factors affect the thermal conductivity beyond simple electron scattering, and such factors play a more important role in tapes with lower thermal conductivity.
