Although the sample preparation did lead to some errors in the data, the huge difference in the resistivity and thermal conductivity between the 20 different commercial REBCO tapes cannot be explained by simple measurement errors. Except for the resistivity in the radial directions of SP1, SP2, and SP3, most of the data did not show any relationship with any parameters of the Cu stabilizer listed in tables 1 and 2. Therefore, other factors dominated the resistivity and thermal conductivity of the tapes. Figure 13 shows a sketch map of the resistivity measurements in the circumferential and radial directions. Then, the corresponding equivalent circuits were established, which helped explore other possible factors. It is worth mentioning that owing to the existence of a superconducting layer, equivalent circuits were much easier. Therefore, we only drew a sketch map and equivalent circuits of the resistivity. The measurement of thermal conductivity is much more complicated and will be briefly discussed. Another point worth mentioning is that in the radial resistivity measurement, the back to face REBCO stack method complicates the current bypass in the back REBCO tape. There are two possible ways: one is from the side of the Cu, and the other is directly going through the Hastelloy. Both this and other studies considered that as the main bypass path is from the side of the Cu, the path through the Hastelloy can be ignored in the current equivalent circuit.
The measurement in the circumferential direction is easier, which is discussed first. This is a simple series circuit. As the Ag layer was deposited by sputtering and the thickness was only a few micrometers, the resistivity of the Ag layer was not the primary factor. The interface resistance between Ag and REBCO was at the level of 10−10 Ω [40], which was also not the dominant factor. The other two possibilities were the Cu layer resistance and interface resistance between Cu and Ag. For the Cu layer, the high resistance could originate from impurities or lose microstructure, as mentioned in a previous study [34]. However, the peak temperature of the thermal conductivity, as shown in table S1, indicates that the purity difference could not be high enough to cause a large difference between the 20 different samples. So if the Cu layer has a high resistance in both electrical and thermal, it may not come from the purity but the microstructure. The interface resistance between Cu and Ag in the REBCO tape has been rarely discussed as yet. According to our private communication with some companies, the companies paid little attention to the Cu layer quality, but only focused on the homogenous thickness. The Transmission Electron Microscope (TEM) image presented in [34] showed that the microstructure of the Cu layer is very loose. It can be deduced that the quality of the Cu and Ag interface is low, resulting in it becoming one of the possible dominant factors of resistance.

