4.3. Application scenarios
In this study, we established a detailed database of the effect of Cu stabilizers on the properties of different commercial

REBCO tapes. The database can support solid data for the REBCO magnet design using the no-insulation method, particularly in the evaluation of the self-protection ability, as we did in section 3.4. Nevertheless, neither the resistivity nor the thermal conductivity was the primary constraints while designing the REBCO magnet. The Ic under the field and the yield strength are very important. Such a database can only play a supporting role in magnet design. However, our database is essential in other application scenarios, such as the superconducting switch and current lead. For the REBCO coil operating at temperatures above the liquid helium temperature, both the switch and current lead cannot be fabricated using NbTi as the most common magnet. In particular, the only choices of materials are REBCO or Bi2223, where the working temperature is over 40 K, that is, a cooled magnet. Until now, few studies have reported the design and REBCO tape selection criteria for either switch or current lead. As neither component is in the center area of the magnet, the applied field is much smaller than that of the main coil. Therefore, the requirement of Ic under field or the yield strength is not as strong as in the main coil, and other criteria become more important. For the switch, it needs a sufficiently high resistance under a normal state, normally at the Ohm level, which requires high resistivity in the circumferential direction. At the same time, the cooled magnet requires high thermal conductivity in the circumferential direction. Normally, samples with high thermal conductivity have low resistivity, such as the most popular tape SP1. However, combined with the data shown in figures 5 and 7, TV2, SN1, and SCS2 still fit this contradictory criterion, while none of them showed any excellent performance in the coil simulation, as shown in section 3.4. The current lead first requires a high current capability. It normally connects to both the main coil and cold screen. The temperature at the cold screen was significantly higher than that of the coil. Therefore, the low thermal conductivity in the circumferential direction can substantially decrease the thermal load at the coil part. It can provide a greater temperature margin to the main coil, which is essential for conducting cooled magnets. Because all the Ic are similar, the low thermal conductivity becomes the most critical criterion of the current lead. SZ1, SZ2, SZ3, SCS1, and SCS5 are promising. Considering that the current leads may not be completely fixed on the main body of the magnet, SCS5 with SS laminating and high yield strength could be the best candidate. Finally, the candidate of neither the switch nor the current lead was tested; it is only an example to select the better commercial tape from our database.
5. Conclusion
